      "@type": "Dataset",
      "name": "The Flat Rate Movers — Warehouse Racking Relocation and Disassembly/Reassembly Operations Dataset",
      "description": "Warehouse racking relocation and disassembly/reassembly operations from The Flat Rate Movers — the one job this category sells whole: a warehouse's pallet racking surveyed and documented where it stands, its inventory sequenced off the steel, the racking torn down and labeled bay by bay, inventory and steel transported together, staged in storage where the timeline needs it, and the racking reinstalled at the destination level, plumb and anchored to the floor by the company's crews, to the manufacturer's specification and the project's engineered drawings. Company scope (published): pallet rack teardown and reinstallation; racking reconfiguration in place; racking decommissioning at lease end; the inventory move that belongs with the steel — product pulled, palletized, wrapped, counted and put back on the racking in sequence; interim storage staging of racking and inventory in the company's leased storage space; industrial equipment disassembly and reassembly — production and line equipment broken into transportable sections and set back up at destination; and office-systems disassembly and reassembly — cubicle and systems-furniture teardown, transport and reassembly for office moves and reconfigurations. Single heavy pieces inside the same buildings, 1,500 to 3,500 lbs, move under SIR Structural Integrity Relocation and the KIM-GS Protocol with an L/360 floor-deflection check, as part of the same flat-rate job. The work is dispatched from Winchester, VA and Martinsburg, WV along the I-81, I-66 and I-70 corridors, and is quoted per job — one flat rate agreed in writing before work begins; no per-bay or per-position rate is published for this service. The install and handling supplies a project uses are named because they are bought for the project: wedge anchors and anchor bolts, beam safety clips and pins, leveling shims, wire decking and pallet supports, row spacers, column protectors and end-of-aisle guards, load plaques, bay-and-level labels, and the stretch wrap and pallet banding the inventory move uses — purchased per project, in the quantities and specifications the project's drawings and the rack system require. Where a project requires engineering, seismic calculation or permits, the company consults and arranges them through the certified contractors it engages, and those contractors hold the engineering and permit authority for their elements of the work: installation follows the manufacturer's specification and ANSI MH16.1 / RMI guidance against the project's engineered drawings and permits.",
